title: the docker images cannot reach an ssh remote at all
updated: 2026-08-11T20:59:42Z
---

A karr board on an ssh:// remote is unusable from the published images, and
the failure reads as a local misconfiguration rather than a missing feature.

Observed against ssh://git@codeberg.org/... :

    Git::Native: ssh host codeberg.org is not in known_hosts. Run
      `ssh-keyscan codeberg.org >> ~/.ssh/known_hosts` ...
      git pull failed: git fetch (CLI fallback) failed:
      error: cannot run ssh: No such file or directory
    fatal: unable to fork

Two independent causes, and the message points at neither:

1. runtime-base installs `git gosu passwd libssl3 libssh2-1 zlib1g libffi8`
   and no openssh-client, so there is no ssh binary. git cannot fork one, and
   the CLI fallback -- which exists precisely for the ssh-config/ProxyCommand
   cases libgit2 cannot do -- can never work for an ssh remote inside the
   image.

2. The README alias (README.md, "Recommended alias for real use") mounts
   .gitconfig, .claude, .codex and .cursor, but neither ~/.ssh nor
   $SSH_AUTH_SOCK, and sets HOME=/home/karr. Git::Native looks for
   $HOME/.ssh/known_hosts, finds nothing, and reports the host as unknown --
   so the advice it prints (`ssh-keyscan ... >> ~/.ssh/known_hosts`) is
   followed on the host and changes nothing inside the container. Even with
   known_hosts present there is still no key and no agent to authenticate
   with.

Neither fix alone helps: an image with openssh-client but no mounted ~/.ssh
still cannot authenticate, and a mounted ~/.ssh without an ssh binary still
cannot fall back.

Wanted:
- openssh-client in runtime-base.
- The documented alias forwards ssh: `-v "$HOME/.ssh:/home/karr/.ssh:ro"` and
  `-v "$SSH_AUTH_SOCK:$SSH_AUTH_SOCK" -e SSH_AUTH_SOCK` (agent preferred over
  mounting private keys; :ro so a container can never write the host keys).
- Consider whether the known_hosts warning should say which HOME it searched,
  since inside a container that is the whole answer.

Found while a real board on codeberg could not sync; the same repo works
natively, where `git ls-remote` over the same URL succeeds.
